\section{Setting up the diagnostic quiz, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the
prose and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you
are reading and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the pre/post-test of \cref{sec:method-instruments}: it sets
the two quizzes up in the learning platform, and it prepares their results
for analysis.
Three files are tangled from this section:
[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]] and [[quiz-knowledge-end.json]], the
descriptions of the two quizzes that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to
Canvas, and [[analyze_quiz.py]], which fetches the results from Canvas and
summarises them.

\subsection{The two quizzes for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-end.json
\end{minted}
The two quizzes share the same knowledge items---the questions that
measure whether a student discerns the critical aspects of
\cref{sec:compilation,sec:errors,sec:markup}---so that the difference
between the start-of-module and end-of-module scores measures what the
module taught.
The quiz titles name LaTeX explicitly, and each is administered once, at
its end of the module.
Because the same questions must measure the gain at the end, the students
see their score but never the correct answers: the items have to stay
fresh between the two administrations.

The start-of-module quiz opens with the consent item, since participation
in the study is opt-in (\cref{sec:method-instruments}) and we ask before
collecting anything.
The knowledge items then follow from position~11 (the numbering is
explained with the items themselves).

<<[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "LaTeX quiz: what you know already",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz checks what you already know about writing documents with LaTeX, so that we can adapt the course. The questions show short pieces of LaTeX source, typeset output, and error messages; if you have not used LaTeX before, answer as well as you can&mdash;guessing is fine, and a low score is expected and completely okay. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers&mdash;the course will teach you those. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 9,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<graded result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<consent item>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

The end-of-module quiz opens with a question of its own instead of the
consent item: whether the student prepared for the quiz.
We ask because preparation would confound the gain measure of
\cref{sec:method-instruments}---a student who reviews the material the
night before measures preparation, not what the module left behind.

<<[[quiz-knowledge-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "LaTeX quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz repeats the LaTeX questions from the start-of-module quiz, so that we can see what you have learned. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 9,
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<graded result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<end-quiz preparation item>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

Both quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single attempt
suffices, and answers are never shuffled (order is meaningful in several
items).
The shared settings come in two chunks---scheduling and interaction---and
each is a flat list of keys, so every brace opens and closes in the chunk
that uses it: the [[quiz_settings]] object opens and closes in the quiz
descriptions above.

<<scheduling settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
@

<<interaction settings>>=
"shuffle_answers": false,
"shuffle_questions": false,
"has_time_limit": false,
"one_at_a_time_type": "none",
"allow_backtracking": true,
"multiple_attempts_enabled": false,
"result_view_restricted": true,
"display_items": true,
"display_item_response": true,
@

Both quizzes show the points awarded---how well the student did---but
neither per-item correctness nor the correct answers, so that the same
questions can measure the gain at the end of the module.

<<graded result view>>=
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true
@

\subsection{The consent and preparation items}
\label{app:quiz-framing-items}

The consent item---kept from the course's earlier diagnostic tests, which
this pre/post-test replaces---asks for study participation before anything
is collected.
It carries no points, and its title doubles as the key under which Canvas
reports the answer, so the analysis program (\cref{app:quiz-analysis})
refers to it by that title.

