\subsection{The knowledge items}
\label{app:quiz-knowledge-items}

The knowledge questions are written for this study and published here in
full.
They walk through the three educational objectives of the module in turn:
the compile model (\cref{sec:compilation}), error handling
(\cref{sec:errors}), and semantic markup (\cref{sec:markup}); each item
tests one candidate critical aspect, each distractor expresses the
behaviour of a learner who has not yet discerned that aspect, and all
weigh one point, so we measure discernment by counting correct answers.
Following \citeauthor{NCOL}'s discussion of assessment, the stems do not
point out the relevant aspect---discerning it is what we measure
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}; each stem shows only LaTeX source, output or an
error message and leaves what matters for the student to see.
The distractors are, throughout, the WYSIWYG-word-processor expectations
the module has to displace: that the document \emph{is} what the screen
shows.

The items are numbered from position~11 so that their placement is
independent of the quiz they appear in: Canvas orders by position, gaps
are fine, and the numbering leaves room for the quiz-specific consent and
preparation items before them.

<<knowledge items>>=
<<item: where the change goes>>,
<<item: after you edit>>,
<<item: spaces in the source>>,
<<item: reading the report>>,
<<item: which error first>>,
<<item: a document that will not compile>>,
<<item: two ways to make a heading>>,
<<item: the same source, another look>>,
<<item: inserting a section>>
@

\subsubsection{The compile model}

Two artifacts, one direction (\cref{sec:compilation}): edits happen in the
source, and the output is derived from it, never the other way round.
The distractors edit the PDF, or expect editing the PDF to change the
source---the WYSIWYG assumption that the document one sees is the document
one edits.

<<item: where the change goes>>=
{
  "position": 11,
  "points_possible": 1,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Where the change goes",
    "item_body": "<p>A document was written in LaTeX and compiled to a PDF. The PDF shows a spelling mistake in a heading: it reads Helllo (with three l's). What is the right way to correct it?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>Correct the letters in the PDF; the source file updates itself to match</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>Correct the spelling in the LaTeX source file and compile it again</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>Correct the letters in the PDF with a PDF editor and hand that PDF in</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>There is nothing to do in the source&mdash;the mistake is only in the PDF</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 2},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

The compiler reads, it does not watch (\cref{sec:compilation}): the output
reflects the last compilation, and nothing changes until the next one.
The distractors expect the PDF to track the source live, as a word
processor's single window does.

<<item: after you edit>>=
{
  "position": 12,
  "points_possible": 1,
  "entry": {
    "title": "After you edit",
    "item_body": "<p>A student changes the title in the LaTeX source and saves the file. The already-open PDF still shows the old title. Which statement is true?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>The save failed&mdash;saving the source should change the PDF at once</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>The change appears in the PDF by itself after a few seconds</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>The source and the PDF are out of sync because of a bug; restart the editor</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>The PDF shows the previous compilation; it changes only when the document is compiled again</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 4},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

Whitespace and characters mean things (\cref{sec:compilation}): the source
is a formal language, so repeated spaces collapse and a blank line starts
a paragraph---the layout of the source is not copied to the output.
The distractors read the source as decorated text whose spacing is
reproduced literally.

