\subsubsection{Errors and debugging the document}

An error message is information (\cref{sec:errors}): it names a place and a
cause.
The correct choice reads both out of the report; the distractors read the
message as something else, or as meaningless.

<<item: reading the report>>=
{
  "position": 14,
  "points_possible": 1,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Reading the report",
    "item_body": "<p>Compiling a document produced the message below. Which statement is true?</p><pre>! Undefined control sequence.\nl.42 \\setion\n            {Introduction}</pre>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>The file could not be found</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>Introduction is not allowed as a section title</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>LaTeX does not recognise the command \\setion (a misspelling of \\section) at about line 42</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>The line number means nothing; the error could be anywhere</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 3},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

The first error counts (\cref{sec:errors}): later errors are often
consequences of the first, so it is fixed first and the document
recompiled.
The distractors take the errors in the wrong order, or as unrelated.

<<item: which error first>>=
{
  "position": 15,
  "points_possible": 1,
  "entry": {
