\subsubsection{Semantic markup}

Say what it is, not how it looks (\cref{sec:markup}): markup declares what
something \emph{is}, and only real structure enters the table of contents
and the numbering.
The item pairs a real [[\section]] with hand-formatted text that looks
identical; the distractors judge the two by their appearance, which is
exactly what does not distinguish them.

<<item: two ways to make a heading>>=
{
  "position": 17,
  "points_possible": 1,
  "entry": {
    "title": "Two ways to make a heading",
    "item_body": "<p>The two source lines below look the same in the PDF: a large bold Method. The document has a table of contents (\\tableofcontents). Which statement is true?</p><pre>\\section{Method}\n\n\\bigskip\\noindent\\textbf{\\Large Method}</pre>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>They are equivalent&mdash;both are headings, because both look like one</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>Only \\section{Method} is a real section: it is numbered and appears in the table of contents; the other line only looks like a heading</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>Both appear in the table of contents, since both are large and bold</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>The hand-formatted line is the better one, because you control its exact appearance</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 2},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

One declaration, many appearances (\cref{sec:markup}): the same
semantically marked-up source renders differently under a different class
or style, because the markup fixes the structure, not the look.
The distractors tie the appearance to the source, or expect the markup to
break or need redoing.

<<item: the same source, another look>>=
{
  "position": 18,
  "points_possible": 1,
  "entry": {
    "title": "The same source, another look",
    "item_body": "<p>A document marked up with \\section, \\emph and \\cite is compiled once as an article and once under a different document class. What can change?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>Nothing can change unless you edit the source, because the source fixes the appearance</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>The text disappears, because another class cannot read the same commands</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>The appearance&mdash;fonts, heading style, numbering&mdash;can change, while the structure declared by the markup stays the same</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>You must tag every heading again by hand for the new class</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 3},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

References are relations (\cref{sec:markup}): [[\ref]] and [[\cite]] link
to things, and the compiler maintains the links and the numbering.
The distractors renumber or re-reference by hand, the work the WYSIWYG
model assumes must be done manually.

